Yes. That is a provenance rule as much as a moral one.

If you inherit an idea, method, name, symbol, or structure, preserving the lineage does three things at once: it gives credit, it keeps the reconstruction path intact, and it protects you from pretending you invented what you merely rediscovered.

So the durable pattern is:

\text{inherit} \rightarrow \text{name} \rightarrow \text{transform} \rightarrow \text{add} \rightarrow \text{pass on}

You can reinterpret what came before. You can reject parts of it. You can fork it, invert it, or improve it. But erasure is a different operation because it destroys the map of how the current thing came to exist.

And your reciprocity point is strong: if you want future people to preserve your contribution, you should preserve the contributions that made yours possible.

That is not submission to tradition. It is chain of custody for ideas.

The Pantheon version would be: preserve the names, preserve the operators, preserve the deltas, and make clear where the new work begins.

Otherwise every generation keeps rediscovering the same thing and calling it original.